#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114933 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbb56f0a-ead0-5794-9e33-7fe0e1f87dc6" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Expressions Involving the Cosecant Function"}] :question/id #uuid "118a33c0-5831-586e-a0a5-e8bd3e1e4c9a" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-3644" :question/problem "Find $f'(x)$ if $f(x) = 2 \\csc x$." :question/worked-solution "Here, we just take the constant out of the derivative and use the result for the derivative of $\\csc x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(2\\csc x) \\\\\n&= 2\\frac{d}{dx}(\\csc x) \\\\\n&= 2(-\\csc x\\cot x) \\\\\n&=-2\\csc x\\cot x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114934 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9699341b-c4e2-547f-8d98-bcda30567272" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Expressions Involving the Cotangent Function"}] :question/id #uuid "23b1cfcf-9f46-5274-b07b-66770a827409" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-3646" :question/problem "Find $\\dfrac {\\textrm d y} {\\textrm d x}$ if $y = 2x - 3\\cot x$." :question/worked-solution "We take the derivative of each term and then add them, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(2x - 3\\cot x) \\\\\n&= 2 - 3(-\\csc^{2} x) \\\\\n&= 2 + 3\\csc^{2} x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114935 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e4b3974-1288-524e-a34c-8091c0e072f1" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Expressions Involving the Secant Function"}] :question/id #uuid "9b2548d6-87fc-56b1-aa7d-cef328eb4e24" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-3647" :question/problem "Calculate $f'(x)$ if $f(x) = x\\sec x$." :question/worked-solution "We use the product rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(x\\sec x) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dx}(x) \\cdot \\sec x + x \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(\\sec x) \\\\\n&= (1) \\cdot \\sec x + x \\cdot (\\sec x\\tan x) \\\\\n&= \\sec x(1 + x\\tan x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114936 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7995b377-1de4-5126-aa29-8cc30b4918f8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent at a Point of a Function Involving Reciprocal Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "83b8a6dd-c8c4-5537-ac88-289265752755" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-3648" :question/problem "Find the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ if $f(x) = \\dfrac{-2\\cot x}{\\sin x}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative using the quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{-2\\cot x}{\\sin x}) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{d}{dx}(-2\\cot x) \\cdot \\sin x - (-2\\cot x) \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(\\sin x))/([\\sin x]^{2}) \\\\\n&= (-2(-\\csc^{2} x) \\cdot \\sin x + 2\\cot x \\cdot (\\cos x))/(\\sin^{2} x) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2\\csc x + 2\\cot x\\cos x}{\\sin^{2} x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nin the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(\\frac{π}{4}) &= (2\\csc (\\frac{π}{4}) + 2\\cot (\\frac{π}{4})\\cos (\\frac{π}{4}))/(\\sin^{2} (\\frac{π}{4})) \\\\\n&= (2 \\cdot (\\sqrt{2}) + 2 \\cdot (1) \\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}))/((\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})^{2}) \\\\\n&= (3\\sqrt{2})/((\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\\n&= 6\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115448 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7995b377-1de4-5126-aa29-8cc30b4918f8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent at a Point of a Function Involving Reciprocal Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81caba8c-b35f-55ea-aa9f-4f7525dc2df3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2} + 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186112132} {:answer/id #uuid "85a93792-6822-5624-9a01-d8eab7517405" :answer/type {:db/ident